Nintendo rocked the world yesterday when  they released the trailer for their new  console dubbed the switch but even  though a lot was shown in the video some  details about the system are still  shrouded in mystery so here are the  seven biggest unanswered questions that  we have about the switch  Nintendo is a reputation of releasing  their consoles at a very competitive  price the Super Nintendo the n64 and  GameCube all launched with $199 price  tags while the Wii and Wii U were  released at 249 and $2.99 respectively  if Nintendo plans to keep with their  tradition of pricing their system's  lower than the competition there's a  chance that we'll see it cost less than  $300 considering that's how much the  Xbox one and ps4 currently cost the  Nintendo switch is being touted as both  a home console and a portable system  which brings up the big question about  battery life according to Nvidia who  crafted a custom Tegra processor made  specifically for the switch the system  will be optimized for efficiency with  the graphics card making it a powerful  yet very efficient console but as with  any handheld the number one thing on  everyone's mind is how long we can go  until we have to plug the system in  the trailer for the switch revealed a  lot about the hardware but also revealed  a lot about the kinds of games that  we'll see on the console as well so far  we can expect Skyrim NBA 2k Mario Mario  Kart and even a new splatoon game but  what else can we expect Nintendo is  notorious for neglecting some franchises  that fans love but also introducing some  great new ones as well but I think a lot  of us can agree that we just want a new  f-zero that's all  while most consoles today are some form  of black white gray or somewhere in  between  some of us long for the days of the  multicolored systems and we might  actually get that with the switch upon  closer inspection of the trailer the  face of the controller seems to be  translucent and not connected to the  handles opening the door for swappable  face plates similar to what Nintendo did  with the 3d s and having this option  could definitely add a little bit flare  to this generation of consoles one of  the main features of the Wii U and it  was announced was the inclusion of a  touchscreen on the controller and while  the switch does have a screen as part of  the controller it's unclear whether or  not it's an actual touchscreen at no  point the trailer does anyone actually  put their finger on it implying that it  might just be another regular monitor  and that might put into question any  theories of backwards compatibility  speaking of which  as much as we all love playing are we  you games on the Wii U there's a big  mystery surrounding  backwards-compatibility on the switch  looking at the trailer the switch  doesn't appear to have any disk drives  as all of the games will be on  cartridges but the last couple of  Nintendo consoles have had at least some  backwards compatibility so the question  remains will the switch be able to play  Wii U games will we be able to transfer  our Wii U purchases over to the switch  will there be a touchscreen and on for  Wii U games  come on Nintendo we gotta know  maybe the most concerning question of  all is if Nintendo sees the switch as  both a home console and a portable one  where will that leave the 3ds while  we're pretty certain that the switch  doesn't have dual screens or 3d  capabilities we do know that it's made  to be taken on the go so that poses the  question is the switch going to be the  all-in-one solution for both home and  portable consoles if Nintendo plans to  merge their eco system into one machine  they're going to have to tell that to  their 60 million 3ds users and I don't  think it'll be pretty those are the  questions about the switch that we want  answered but I'm sure there are  countless others that you're just dying  to ask let me know in the comments below  and for more coverage about the switch  and all your other favorite consoles  stay tuned the game spots
 

